WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250 line:88% position:50% size:90%
A smooth sentence can hide an important

2
00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:03.140 line:88% position:50% size:90%
assessment problem.

3
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.750 line:88% position:50% size:90%
The speaker may have changed words, avoided

4
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.690 line:88% position:50% size:90%
a situation, or asked someone else to

5
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:08.350 line:88% position:50% size:90%
speak.

6
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.730 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Fluency assessment therefore needs both the
speech

7
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.560 line:88% position:50% size:90%
sample and the person's experience.

8
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.560 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Stuttering can include repetitions,
prolongations,

9
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:16.840 line:88% position:50% size:90%
blocks, and broken words.

10
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.210 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Cluttering can involve a rapid or irregular

11
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:21.770 line:88% position:50% size:90%
rate, atypical prosody, and reduced clarity in

12
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:22.580 line:88% position:50% size:90%
connected speech.

13
00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.180 line:88% position:50% size:90%
The two can coexist.

14
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.110 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Fast speech alone is not a complete

15
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:26.770 line:88% position:50% size:90%
diagnosis.

16
00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.480 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Start with the history and choose samples

17
00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.070 line:88% position:50% size:90%
that represent the person's life.

18
00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:34.900 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Ask about onset, family history, variation,
and

19
00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:35.750 line:88% position:50% size:90%
prior therapy.

20
00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:37.970 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Also ask about the person's reactions.

21
00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.810 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Identify situations that are easier or harder.

22
00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.240 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Gather speech across more than one session

23
00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:43.860 line:88% position:50% size:90%
and setting.

24
00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.720 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Conversation, narrative, and reading can place
different

25
00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:47.750 line:88% position:50% size:90%
demands on the speaker.

26
00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.720 line:88% position:50% size:90%
A short successful clinic exchange cannot
stand

27
00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:52.140 line:88% position:50% size:90%
in for all of those situations.

28
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.160 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Keep the context attached to the sample

29
00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:56.160 line:88% position:50% size:90%
so that a change in performance remains

30
00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:56.820 line:88% position:50% size:90%
interpretable.

31
00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.080 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Now follow the counting rule.

32
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.570 line:88% position:50% size:90%
In the course method, a repeated unit

33
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.150 line:88% position:50% size:90%
is counted as one repetition event, rather

34
00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.490 line:88% position:50% size:90%
than counting each repeated attempt as a

35
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:07.180 line:88% position:50% size:90%
separate event.

36
00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.410 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Suppose a sample contains one hundred words

37
00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.120 line:88% position:50% size:90%
and ten disfluency events.

38
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.970 line:88% position:50% size:90%
The total disfluency index is ten percent.

39
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.500 line:88% position:50% size:90%
If six of those events are repetitions,

40
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.750 line:88% position:50% size:90%
the repetition index is six percent of

41
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.030 line:88% position:50% size:90%
the words, while repetitions make up sixty

42
00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.320 line:88% position:50% size:90%
percent of the disfluencies.

43
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:25.010 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Same six events, different denominator,
different

44
00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:25.670 line:88% position:50% size:90%
question.

45
00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:28.350 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Frequency is only one dimension.

46
00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:31.200 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Measure duration, describe the types, and
consider

47
00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.790 line:88% position:50% size:90%
speaking rate with and without disfluencies.

48
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.730 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Then look beyond the speech stream.

49
00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:38.410 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Associated movements, physical responses,
avoidance,

50
00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.880 line:88% position:50% size:90%
embarrassment, and participation can change
the meaning

51
00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:42.270 line:88% position:50% size:90%
of an apparently low count.

52
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.480 line:88% position:50% size:90%
A person who speaks less or relies

53
00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.960 line:88% position:50% size:90%
on a partner may reduce observable
disfluencies

54
00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.170 line:88% position:50% size:90%
while still experiencing a substantial
problem.

55
00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:50.920 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Ask what the speaker is doing and

56
00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:52.130 line:88% position:50% size:90%
what the speaker is giving up.

57
00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:56.190 line:88% position:50% size:90%
The slides list numerical considerations for
stuttering:

58
00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:59.000 line:88% position:50% size:90%
a total index of ten percent, relevant

59
00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.100 line:88% position:50% size:90%
type indexes of three percent, and an

60
00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:02.750 line:88% position:50% size:90%
average duration of one second.

61
00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:05.720 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Learn these as the course's assessment
considerations,

62
00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.530 line:88% position:50% size:90%
alongside the prevalent types, secondary
behaviors,

63
00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.260 line:88% position:50% size:90%
and client or parent concern.

64
00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:12.220 line:88% position:50% size:90%
They are not a substitute for integrating

65
00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:13.100 line:88% position:50% size:90%
the whole assessment.

66
00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:16.360 line:88% position:50% size:90%
For suspected cluttering, compare structured
articulation

67
00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.060 line:88% position:50% size:90%
performance with spontaneous speech, and
consider

68
00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:22.010 line:88% position:50% size:90%
rate, prosody, coarticulation, and awareness.

69
00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.420 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Finally, examine responsiveness to support.

70
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.490 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Stimulability asks how performance changes
when an

71
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.970 line:88% position:50% size:90%
appropriate technique is tried, starting at a

72
00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.300 line:88% position:50% size:90%
simpler level and increasing complexity.

73
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.170 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Show the change in the task and

74
00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.130 line:88% position:50% size:90%
the response, rather than assuming one good

75
00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.840 line:88% position:50% size:90%
attempt settles the diagnosis.

76
00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.270 line:88% position:50% size:90%
The assessment should explain the pattern, its

77
00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.860 line:88% position:50% size:90%
context, and its impact.

78
00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.500 line:88% position:50% size:90%
Counts describe the sample.

79
00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.120 line:88% position:50% size:90%
The combined evidence explains the person's
needs.
